{"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArpeggiate\/id","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"kind":"symbol","hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArpeggiate"]]},"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Instance Property","role":"symbol","title":"id","modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"symbolKind":"property","fragments":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"id","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"String","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:SS"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"externalID":"s:7IvorMXL13MXLArpeggiateV2idSSSgvp"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"let","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"id","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"String","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:SS"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["macOS"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Discussion","anchor":"discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Maps to the MusicXML ","type":"text"},{"code":"id","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" attribute.","type":"text"}]}]}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarpeggiate\/id"]}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The optional-unique-id attribute group allows an element to optionally specify an ID that is unique to the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"entire document. This attribute group is not used for a required id attribute, or for an id attribute that"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"specifies an id reference."}],"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL":{"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","role":"collection","title":"IvorMXL","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A MusicXML parser, normalizer, validator, and formatter."}]},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLArpeggiate/id":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"id"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"String","preciseIdentifier":"s:SS"},{"kind":"text","text":"?"}],"title":"id","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The optional-unique-id attribute group allows an element to optionally specify an ID that is unique to the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"entire document. This attribute group is not used for a required id attribute, or for an id attribute that"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"specifies an id reference."}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarpeggiate\/id","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArpeggiate\/id","role":"symbol"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLArpeggiate":{"type":"topic","title":"MXLArpeggiate","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLArpeggiate","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlarpeggiate","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLArpeggiate","role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLArpeggiate","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The arpeggiate type indicates that this note is part of an arpeggiated chord. The number attribute can be used"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"to distinguish between two simultaneous chords arpeggiated separately (different numbers) or together (same"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"number). The direction attribute is used if there is an arrow on the arpeggio sign. By default, arpeggios go"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"from the lowest to highest note. The length of the sign can be determined from the position attributes for the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"arpeggiate elements used with the top and bottom notes of the arpeggiated chord. If the unbroken attribute is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"set to yes, it indicates that the arpeggio continues onto another staff within the part. This serves as a hint"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"to applications and is not required for cross-staff arpeggios."}]}}}